281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 50 0 R
/T (enterprise requirements: performance and scalability, complex business logic, system integration, extreme UX customisation, security and governance, AI / ML and advanced technology, vendor or platform dependency, and heavy data transformations. It also showed how a three-tier hybrid architecture of a low-code application, pro-code extensions and enterprise systems can address requirements beyond standard low-code capabilities.)
/E (enterprise requirements: performance and scalability, complex business logic, system integration, extreme UX customisation, security and governance, AI / ML and advanced technology, vendor or platform dependency, and heavy data transformations. It also showed how a three-tier hybrid architecture of a low-code application, pro-code extensions and enterprise systems can address requirements beyond standard low-code capabilities.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 50 0 R
/T (Low-code platforms offer speed and simplicity for standard business applications, but their limitations become more significant as enterprise complexity increases. The trade-off is clear: low-code gives fast delivery, lower cost and wider participation in development, in exchange for ceilings in scale, logic, integration, customisation, governance and platform independence. For the Student Admission & Applicant Management System, the standard functionality can be built quickly with low-code, while demanding requirements in logic, integration, security, scale and advanced technology need pro-code extensions connected to core enterprise systems. A hybrid architecture keeps the speed of low-code for the standard layer and adds the flexibility of custom code where it is needed, which is why it is regarded as the practical standard for scalable enterprise platforms. It is therefore important to understand the limitations of a platform before selecting a development approach, so that the chosen approach matches the requirements of the enterprise application.)
/E (Low-code platforms offer speed and simplicity for standard business applications, but their limitations become more significant as enterprise complexity increases. The trade-off is clear: low-code gives fast delivery, lower cost and wider participation in development, in exchange for ceilings in scale, logic, integration, customisation, governance and platform independence. For the Student Admission & Applicant Management System, the standard functionality can be built quickly with low-code, while demanding requirements in logic, integration, security, scale and advanced technology need pro-code extensions connected to core enterprise systems. A hybrid architecture keeps the speed of low-code for the standard layer and adds the flexibility of custom code where it is needed, which is why it is regarded as the practical standard for scalable enterprise platforms. It is therefore important to understand the limitations of a platform before selecting a development approach, so that the chosen approach matches the requirements of the enterprise application.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
